Loops are really convenience macros in languages layered on top of branches and jumps.
Heres how they work:


for(INIT; CONTINUITY_CHECK; POSTEXECUTION_EVENT) {
  BODY
}

while (CONTINUITY_CHECK) {
  BODY
}

The are both translated similarly as below. Of course for while loops you won't
have INIT or POSTEXECUTION_EVENT but that apart they are exactly the same.

----------------------------------------------
     	  INIT
          if (!CONTINUITY_CHECK) jmp end 
	  ;; OR  jmp LATCH

START:
          BODY ....
	  BODY ....

	  POSTEXECUTION_EVENT
LATCH:	  if (CONTINUITY_CHECK)  jmp START

END:
----------------------------------------------


As you can see the first jmp is taken only on the first
iteration. If compiler can prove the first iteration will
happen unconditionally it can be removed. 
       e.g., for( i = 0; i < 0x1000; i++) // can remove

       	     for( i = (uint32_t)random(); i < 0x1000; i++) // can't remove

        
The second jmp at the bottom is the backward jump. Based on static BP on most
microprocessors, forward jumps are not taken and backward jumps are taken.
So you should get good prediction with this format.


